# Every text file is LF, on disk and in the repository, on every platform.
# `text=auto` alone still lets core.autocrlf convert to CRLF on a Windows
# checkout, which is exactly what makes git print "warning: LF will be
# replaced by CRLF" on every add - it is flagging that mismatch between
# the working copy and what a fresh checkout would produce. Pinning eol=lf
# removes the mismatch instead of just explaining it: checkout matches
# storage matches working copy, on every platform, and the warning has
# nothing left to report.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out so nothing above can ever misdetect one of these and attempt
# a line-ending conversion that would corrupt it.
*.png    binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff2  binary
*.bin    binary
*.onnx   binary

# The shell twins must keep LF whatever a Windows checkout would prefer: a
# .sh with CRLF line endings fails on a Linux runner with a "bad
# interpreter" error naming a shebang that looks perfectly correct in an
# editor. `* text=auto eol=lf` above already does this; naming them makes
# it survive somebody relaxing that line.
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Fettler's own tests write fixture files and compare what comes back byte
# for byte - line endings included, because preserving a file's existing
# endings through an edit is one of the things Fettler promises. A checkout
# that rewrote them would change what the fixtures test.
Fettler.Tests/files/** text eol=lf
burler.Tests/files/**  text eol=lf
